1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
The 2026 syllabus named five application protocols. The 2027 syllabus names two.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The three that left are still the three jobs of the application layer: send, move a file, fetch a mailbox.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
For 2027 this lesson is class C: outside the named clause, inside the same layer.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Three questions survive the change: who opens the connection, how many round trips, how many bytes.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
The client composes in a mail user agent and submits to a submission server on port 587.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Each hop between two servers is one SMTP session on port 25, and one TCP connection.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
A submission and two relays make three sessions, and each receiving server adds one Received header.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The last server hands the message to a mailbox, and the client fetches it on 110 or 143.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The server greets with 220. The client says EHLO and is told what the server can do, answered 250.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
MAIL FROM names the sender and RCPT TO names one recipient each. Both are answered 250.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
DATA earns 354, the one intermediate reply: send the message, and end it with a line holding a single dot.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The dot earns 250 and QUIT earns 221. Six exchanges, plus one per recipient.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Handshake, EHLO, MAIL, DATA, body, QUIT: six round trips before any recipient is counted.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
One round trip per recipient: one recipient gives seven, three give nine, five give eleven.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Connections stay at one. More recipients do not open more connections.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Three recipients at three domains: a submission of nine plus three relays of seven is thirty round trips over four connections.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
RCPT TO is the envelope: three To, one Cc and two Bcc make six recipient commands.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The headers show four addresses. Bcc shows none, and that is the whole purpose of the field.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
MAIL FROM is where a bounce goes. From: is only what the reader sees, and they may differ.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
A relay may add Received and Return-Path. It must not change the envelope it was given.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
The first digit is the class: two is success, three is intermediate, four is temporary, five is permanent.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Four means retry the same message later. Five means do not retry: bounce it to the sender.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Five queued messages: 250 accepted, 451 and 421 retried, 550 and 552 bounced.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The second digit narrows the reason: zero is syntax, two is connection, five is mail system status.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Port 21 carries the commands for the whole session, and it stays open until the client quits.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Each transfer opens a second connection for the bytes, and closes it when the transfer ends.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
One file: two connections, six round trips. Five files: six connections, eighteen round trips.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Three files and a directory listing: four transfers, so five connections and fifteen round trips.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Active mode: the client sends PORT, and the server dials out from port 20.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Passive mode: the client sends PASV, the server answers 227 with an address, and the client dials.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Behind a firewall the client cannot be dialled, so passive mode exists to make both connections outgoing.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The control connection is stateful: the current directory, the transfer type and the login all live on the server.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Mail data is seven-bit text, so a binary attachment must be encoded before it is sent.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Base64 turns three bytes into four characters, so the size grows by a third.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
One million bytes becomes 1,333,336 characters, in 17,544 lines of at most seventy-six characters.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Each line carries two more bytes, so 1,368,424 bytes go on the wire: an overhead of 36.8 per cent.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
POP3 downloads a message and deletes it from the server. The mailbox then lives on the client.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
IMAP keeps the mailbox on the server and synchronises flags, so two devices see one mailbox.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
A hundred messages of 20,000 bytes: 2,000,000 bytes by POP3, and 110,000 by IMAP reading headers and three bodies.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Four steps: name the server and its port, open the connection, run the dialog, then count the unit the question asked for.

